In this lesson we'll look at how to describe the shape of a data set. When you compare data sets, the shape of the distribution tells a story. First, symmetric. That means the left and right sides mirror each other about the centre, so the mean and the median sit roughly in the same spot. Think of the heights of a large random sample of adults. Next, skewed. A skewed distribution has one tail that stretches out further than the other. If the long tail is on the right, we call it right-skewed, and the mean gets pulled to the right of the median. Household income is a classic example. If the long tail is on the left, it's left-skewed, and the mean sits to the left of the median, like the age at retirement. Then there's bi-modal, which means you see two clear peaks instead of one. This usually suggests the data is really a mix of two groups, like commute times combining walkers and drivers. When comparing two data sets, check the centre, the spread, and the shape. Now let's see it in action.